🔍 Monitoring vs. Observability — What’s the Real Difference?

If you’re building or maintaining distributed systems, you’ve probably heard both terms thrown around — sometimes even interchangeably. But they serve very different purposes.

Here’s how I break it down:

👁 Monitoring
➤ Goal: Tell you if something is broken
➤ Based on predefined metrics, alerts, and thresholds
➤ Reactive by nature (e.g., “CPU > 80% — raise alarm”)
➤ Tools: Prometheus, Grafana, CloudWatch

🧠 Observability
➤ Goal: Help you understand why it’s broken
➤ Involves digging into metrics, logs, traces — often in real time
➤ Proactive and investigative
➤ Tools: OpenTelemetry, Jaeger, Honeycomb, Datadog

🚨 TL;DR:
Monitoring = “Is it broken?”
Observability = “Why is it broken?”

In modern systems, observability isn’t just nice-to-have — it’s critical. Especially when things go wrong in ways you didn’t anticipate.

How does your team handle this today? Still relying on dashboards and alerts, or moving toward full-stack observability?

